# Common CSS Flex Properties

Flex properties control how elements behave inside a flex layout.

Some of the most commonly used Flexbox properties are:

* `flex-wrap`
* `flex-flow`
* `justify-content`
* `align-items`
* `align-self`


# Wrapping Items with `flex-wrap`

The `flex-wrap` property determines how flex items are wrapped within a flex container to fit the available space.

`flex-wrap` has three possible values:

* `nowrap`
* `wrap`
* `wrap-reverse`


## `nowrap`

`nowrap` is the default value.

Flex items will not be wrapped onto a new line, even if their combined width exceeds the width of the container.

Example HTML:

    <main>
      <div id="first-div"></div>
      <div id="second-div"></div>
      <div id="third-div"></div>
    </main>

Example CSS:

    main {
      width: 200px;
      display: flex;
      border: 2px solid #444;
    }

    div {
      width: 80px;
      height: 50px;
    }

    #first-div {
      background-color: #4d70b2;
    }

    #second-div {
      background-color: #5c4db2;
    }

    #third-div {
      background-color: #4da3b2;
    }

The container has a width of `200px`.

The three child elements have a combined width of:

`80px + 80px + 80px = 240px`

The width of the div elements exceeds the width of the container.

Because `nowrap` is the default, the flex items will be shrunk to fit the available space.


## `wrap`

If you want flex items to move onto a new line when they exceed the width of their container, use:

    flex-wrap: wrap;

Example:

    main {
      width: 200px;
      display: flex;
      flex-wrap: wrap;
      border: 2px solid #444;
    }

    div {
      width: 80px;
      height: 50px;
    }

    #first-div {
      background-color: #4d70b2;
    }

    #second-div {
      background-color: #5c4db2;
    }

    #third-div {
      background-color: #4da3b2;
    }

HTML:

    <main>
      <div id="first-div"></div>
      <div id="second-div"></div>
      <div id="third-div"></div>
    </main>

With `flex-wrap: wrap`, the flex items will be rearranged into multiple rows when they don't fit on one line.

Instead of shrinking to fit, the items can move onto a new line.


## `wrap-reverse`

You can wrap flex items in the reverse direction with:

    flex-wrap: wrap-reverse;

`wrap-reverse` works like `wrap`, but the wrapped lines are placed in the opposite direction.

The three values are:

* `nowrap` = Items do not wrap.
* `wrap` = Items wrap normally.
* `wrap-reverse` = Items wrap in the reverse direction.


# Combining Direction and Wrap with `flex-flow`

The `flex-flow` property is a shorthand property for:

* `flex-direction`
* `flex-wrap`

Instead of writing:

    flex-direction: column;
    flex-wrap: wrap-reverse;

You can write:

    flex-flow: column wrap-reverse;

Example:

    <main>
      <div id="first-div"></div>
      <div id="second-div"></div>
      <div id="third-div"></div>
    </main>

CSS:

    main {
      width: 200px;
      height: 120px;
      display: flex;
      flex-flow: column wrap-reverse;
      border: 2px solid #444;
    }

    div {
      width: 80px;
      height: 50px;
    }

    #first-div {
      background-color: #4d70b2;
    }

    #second-div {
      background-color: #5c4db2;
    }

    #third-div {
      background-color: #4da3b2;
    }

In this example:

    flex-flow: column wrap-reverse;

is equivalent to:

    flex-direction: column;
    flex-wrap: wrap-reverse;


## `flex-flow` Syntax

The general syntax is:

    flex-flow: <flex-direction> <flex-wrap>;

Examples:

    flex-flow: row nowrap;

    flex-flow: row wrap;

    flex-flow: row-reverse wrap;

    flex-flow: column wrap;

    flex-flow: column wrap-reverse;


# Part 1 Quick Summary

## `flex-wrap`

Controls whether flex items wrap onto new lines.

    flex-wrap: nowrap;

* Default value.
* Items do not wrap.
* Items can shrink to fit the available space.

    flex-wrap: wrap;

* Items wrap onto new lines when necessary.

    flex-wrap: wrap-reverse;

* Items wrap onto new lines in the reverse direction.


## `flex-flow`

A shorthand property for:

* `flex-direction`
* `flex-wrap`

Example:

    flex-flow: column wrap-reverse;

is equivalent to:

    flex-direction: column;
    flex-wrap: wrap-reverse;


# Key Idea

`flex-wrap` controls whether flex items wrap.

`flex-flow` controls both the direction and wrapping of flex items.

Remember:

    flex-flow = flex-direction + flex-wrap


# Common CSS Flex Properties

# Controlling Alignment with justify-content

* The `justify-content` property determines how flex items are distributed along the **main axis** of a flex container.
* The main axis depends on the value of `flex-direction`.
* For the default `flex-direction: row`, the main axis runs horizontally from left to right.

## flex-start

* `flex-start` positions all flex items at the beginning of the main axis.
* This is the default value.

    .container {
      display: flex;
      justify-content: flex-start;
    }

## flex-end

* `flex-end` positions all flex items at the end of the main axis.

    .container {
      display: flex;
      justify-content: flex-end;
    }

## center

* `center` positions the flex items in the center of the main axis.

    .container {
      display: flex;
      justify-content: center;
    }

## space-between

* `space-between` distributes the flex items evenly along the main axis.
* The first item is placed at the beginning.
* The last item is placed at the end.
* The remaining space is distributed evenly between the items.
* There is no extra space between the container's edges and the first or last item.

    .container {
      display: flex;
      justify-content: space-between;
    }

## space-around

* `space-around` distributes equal space around each flex item.
* The space between two items is twice the space between an item and the container's edge.

    .container {
      display: flex;
      justify-content: space-around;
    }

## space-evenly

* `space-evenly` distributes the available space equally between all flex items and the container's edges.
* The spacing between items is the same as the spacing between the items and the container's edges.

    .container {
      display: flex;
      justify-content: space-evenly;
    }

# Controlling Cross-Axis Alignment with align-items

* The `align-items` property controls how flex items are aligned along the **cross axis**.
* If `flex-direction` is `row`, the cross axis runs vertically.
* If `flex-direction` is `column`, the cross axis runs horizontally.

## center

* `center` aligns the flex items in the center of the cross axis.

    .container {
      display: flex;
      align-items: center;
    }

## flex-start

* `flex-start` aligns the flex items at the beginning of the cross axis.

    .container {
      display: flex;
      align-items: flex-start;
    }

## flex-end

* `flex-end` aligns the flex items at the end of the cross axis.

    .container {
      display: flex;
      align-items: flex-end;
    }

## stretch

* `stretch` stretches the flex items across the cross axis when possible.
* This is the default value for `align-items`.
* For stretching to work, the items generally need to have `auto` sizing on the cross-axis dimension.

    .container {
      display: flex;
      align-items: stretch;
    }

# Quick Summary

* `justify-content` controls alignment along the **main axis**.
* `align-items` controls alignment along the **cross axis**.
* With `flex-direction: row`:
  * Main axis = horizontal.
  * Cross axis = vertical.
* With `flex-direction: column`:
  * Main axis = vertical.
  * Cross axis = horizontal.

# Common Values

* `justify-content`:
  * `flex-start`
  * `flex-end`
  * `center`
  * `space-between`
  * `space-around`
  * `space-evenly`

* `align-items`:
  * `flex-start`
  * `flex-end`
  * `center`
  * `stretch`

# Key Idea

* `justify-content` answers: **How should the items be distributed along the main axis?**
* `align-items` answers: **How should the items be aligned along the cross axis?**


# Common CSS Flex Properties

# Aligning Individual Items with align-self

* The `align-self` property allows you to override the `align-items` value for an individual flex item.
* While `align-items` controls the alignment of **all flex items** in a container, `align-self` lets you control the alignment of **one specific item**.
* It works along the **cross axis**.

## Example

    .container {
      display: flex;
      align-items: center;
    }

    .item-1 {
      align-self: flex-start;
    }

* In this example:
  * The container centers all items using `align-items: center`.
  * `.item-1` overrides that setting with `align-self: flex-start`.
  * The other items remain centered.
  * Only `.item-1` is moved to the beginning of the cross axis.

# Common align-self Values

## stretch

* `stretch` stretches the individual flex item across the cross axis when possible.
* This is the default value of `align-self`.

    .item {
      align-self: stretch;
    }

## center

* `center` places the individual flex item in the center of the cross axis.

    .item {
      align-self: center;
    }

## flex-start

* `flex-start` places the individual flex item at the beginning of the cross axis.

    .item {
      align-self: flex-start;
    }

## flex-end

* `flex-end` places the individual flex item at the end of the cross axis.

    .item {
      align-self: flex-end;
    }

# align-items vs align-self

* `align-items` affects **all flex items** inside the flex container.

    .container {
      display: flex;
      align-items: center;
    }

* `align-self` affects **one specific flex item**.

    .item {
      align-self: flex-end;
    }

* If an item has an `align-self` value, it can override the container's `align-items` setting for that item.

# Flex Properties Summary

* `flex-wrap`
  * Controls whether flex items stay on one line or wrap onto multiple lines.
  * Common values: `nowrap`, `wrap`, `wrap-reverse`.

* `flex-flow`
  * A shorthand for `flex-direction` and `flex-wrap`.
  * Example: `flex-flow: row wrap;`

* `justify-content`
  * Controls how items are distributed along the **main axis**.
  * Common values: `flex-start`, `flex-end`, `center`, `space-between`, `space-around`, `space-evenly`.

* `align-items`
  * Controls how all flex items are aligned along the **cross axis**.
  * Common values: `stretch`, `flex-start`, `flex-end`, `center`.

* `align-self`
  * Controls how one individual flex item is aligned along the **cross axis**.
  * Common values: `stretch`, `flex-start`, `flex-end`, `center`.

# Main Axis vs Cross Axis

* The direction of the axes depends on `flex-direction`.

## flex-direction: row

    .container {
      display: flex;
      flex-direction: row;
    }

* Main axis = horizontal.
* Cross axis = vertical.

## flex-direction: column

    .container {
      display: flex;
      flex-direction: column;
    }

* Main axis = vertical.
* Cross axis = horizontal.

# Final Key Points

* `flex-wrap` controls whether items wrap.
* `flex-flow` combines `flex-direction` and `flex-wrap`.
* `justify-content` controls distribution along the main axis.
* `align-items` controls alignment of all items along the cross axis.
* `align-self` overrides `align-items` for an individual item.
* Understanding the main axis and cross axis is essential for using Flexbox effectively.

# Quick Reference

| Property | Controls | Common Values |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `flex-wrap` | Item wrapping | `nowrap`, `wrap`, `wrap-reverse` |
| `flex-flow` | Direction + wrapping | `row wrap`, `column nowrap` |
| `justify-content` | Main-axis distribution | `flex-start`, `center`, `space-between` |
| `align-items` | Cross-axis alignment of all items | `stretch`, `center`, `flex-start` |
| `align-self` | Cross-axis alignment of one item | `stretch`, `center`, `flex-end` |

# Key Idea

* Flexbox becomes much easier when you remember:
  * `justify-content` = **main axis**
  * `align-items` = **cross axis for all items**
  * `align-self` = **cross axis for one item**
  * `flex-wrap` = **whether items can move onto additional lines**
  * `flex-flow` = **direction + wrapping**
